FAIR NOTE ON LOGIND THEORY (DOWNGRADE)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

At this point, acknowledged to user:
"You're right to be skeptical. Logind itself probably ISN'T the root
cause of 49% IO pressure — a few hundred log lines don't do that.
But logind being in a storm at the exact moment of the IO spike is
still significant."

Most likely reading at this point:
- Something causes a real IO stall
- During the stall, systemd-logind's timer-driven suspend attempts queue up
- When IO unblocks, they all drain at once
- Lid storm is a MARKER of the stall, not the CAUSE

This was closer to truth but still incomplete.

